Documented interval semantics (bedtools intersect docs):
- `-split`: "spliced" BAM / blocked BED12 entries are treated as distinct intervals; overlap computed between blocks of A and blocks of B.
- `-f X`: require minimum overlap as fraction of A.
- Both combined: fraction = (sum of block overlaps) / (sum of A block lengths).

The sweep-level span filter is an approximation that must not wrongly EXCLUDE candidates when -split is on. Since block overlap ≤ span overlap, and A block sum ≤ A span..., the span-level filter with -f is NOT a conservative superset: span-level fraction can be smaller than block-level fraction (because denominator is larger). It IS conservative w.r.t. overlapB: span-level overlapB = spanOverlap/bLen... no wait bLen is the span of B; hits are dropped if either fraction fails (non-either mode). So span-filter can wrongly drop hits whose block-fraction qualifies. So a correct fix must ensure candidate generation doesn't drop those.

